{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/claude-code-settings.json",
  "_comment": "claude-hub project permissions. Phase 1.5 of --dangerously-skip-permissions migration (Issue #53). Phase 1 (initial allowlist) was too restrictive for /pdca workflow — read-only commands (cat/head/tail), runtime invocations (bash/bun/node/python/npx), and broader git operations (diff/show/log/checkout/stash/commit/push) are now allowed. Destructive ops (sudo/rm-rf/dd/mkfs), network exfil (curl/wget/nc/ssh/scp), and shell injection (eval/exec) remain denied. The hijoguchi Bot (scripts/start-hijoguchi.sh) auto-loads this file because its CWD = claude-hub root. Phase 2 will flip CLAUDE_HUB_UNSAFE_SKIP_PERMISSIONS default from 1 to 0 and the allow/deny entries below become the active policy.",
  "_known_limitations": "In enforce mode the deny list is best-effort; Claude Code's allow/deny patterns are matched as command-line prefixes before shell expansion. Concrete consequences: (1) `rm -rf ~/foo` (tilde literal) and `rm /Users/...` (absolute) require separate patterns. (2) **Flag-order bypass**: positional deny like `Bash(git push -f:*)` does NOT match `git push origin main -f` because the flag is not at the prefix position. The same applies to `git reset --hard`, `git push --force`, `rm -rf` — an attacker (or careless reorder) can re-position destructive flags to evade the deny. (3) **Interpreter bypass (KNOWN+ACCEPTED for Phase 1.5)**: `Bash(bash:*)`, `Bash(python:*)`, `Bash(node:*)`, `Bash(bun:*)` etc are in allow. Claude Code only matches the parent `Bash(...)` invocation, not child processes spawned by the interpreter. So `bash -c 'curl exfil-url'` reaches curl despite `Bash(curl:*)` being denied. This is intentionally accepted because /pdca workflow needs bash to invoke hooks (e.g. `bash hooks/journey-ac-check.sh`), bun for tests, and python for scripts. Removing interpreter allows would block /pdca. Phase 2 will reconsider with sandboxing or AST-level inspection. (4) `Read` deny rules cover file-reading via the Read tool. **Bash viewers (cat/head/tail/less/more) have explicit path-based denies for known secret paths (.env / ~/.aws / ~/.ssh / credentials / id_rsa / id_ed25519)** so common exfil paths are blocked even though `Bash(cat:*)` itself is in allow. Tighten per Phase 2 review before flipping the default; until then, treat the deny list as a pit-of-success guide rather than a hard guarantee.",
